WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
INTRO:
Ever since the pandemic started and Jerome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.160
Powell started the money printer, the stock market&nbsp;
has only gone one direction up. This has trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.120
millions of retail investors who started investing&nbsp;
during the pandemic to constantly buy the dip.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:19.680
If a stock sells of 3 to 5% it’s time to buy and&nbsp;
if a stock sells off 10% it’s time to go all in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.120
Ironically, when people buy these quote-on-quote&nbsp;
discounts, they’re simply investing at all-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.880
high levels from a few days or weeks ago. So,&nbsp;
it’s not like they’re really getting a discount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.800
but we can’t really blame them because this is&nbsp;
all they’ve seen. In fact, throughout all of 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.640
the S&amp;P 500 didn’t even experience a single 10%&nbsp;
correction. The largest sell-off the index saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.160
last year wasn’t even 6%. So, it makes sense&nbsp;
that many retail investors have been trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:54.160
to go all-in on even the slightest of pullbacks.&nbsp;
Many are even claiming that this is simply the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.680
new normal and that we’ll never see another&nbsp;
40 or 50% correction in the stock market. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.800
this is actually the most dangerous sentiment to&nbsp;
have because the tops of bubbles are literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:11.840
called the “New Paradigm!!”. So, here’s the&nbsp;
truth about stocks that don’t come back.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.640
THE DISRUPTORS:&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.880
Starting off the list, we have the innovative&nbsp;
companies or the growth companies that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.560
dominated the market over the past 2 years. Often,&nbsp;
these companies promise to revolutionize how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.040
do everyday tasks. For example, instead&nbsp;
of building up a strong network of taxis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.000
that’s easily accessible, these companies will&nbsp;
promise to provide you with self-driving cars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.400
Or instead of building more stores and carrying&nbsp;
more inventory close to where you live, they’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.440
promise to deliver any product straight to your&nbsp;
doorstep. Generally, these companies have the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.800
of intentions, but they don’t have the best of&nbsp;
balance sheets, so it just takes a simple market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:55.440
downturn to wipe them out completely. Take Mark&nbsp;
Cuban’s Broadcast.com for example. In the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.800
Broadcast.com allowed users to stream audio and&nbsp;
eventually even video through the internet. Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.720
that most of us spend hours streaming podcasts,&nbsp;
music, videos, and movies today, this was clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.760
a great idea. And multiple hundred billion dollar&nbsp;
companies have been built based on just streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.440
one or two types of media. Yahoo could see this&nbsp;
future in 1999, and that’s why they agreed to buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:24.080
Broadcast.com for $5.7 billion. But, even Yahoo&nbsp;
couldn’t sustain the company through the dotcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.880
crash, and they ended up shutting down one sector&nbsp;
after another until they shut down the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:36.320
company. Another big bust from the dotcom era is&nbsp;
Pets.com. The idea of Pets.com was simple: sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.560
pet toys and food through the internet to make&nbsp;
these products more accessible and convenient than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:46.640
ever before. They had big investors on board like&nbsp;
Amazon who at one point owned half the company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.200
This allowed Pets.com to complete an extremely&nbsp;
successful IPO raising over $300 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.720
Yet, just 268 days later, they went&nbsp;
bankrupt thanks to the dotcom crash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:03.520
Now, Pets.com wasn’t a bad idea by any means.&nbsp;
Chewy basically executed the same concept a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:10.000
decade later and they’re worth nearly $20 billion&nbsp;
today. So, clearly, the idea doesn’t really matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.960
when we’re talking about a stock market crash.&nbsp;
The only thing that matters during a downturn is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.600
the balance sheet because if a company can’t&nbsp;
survive the downturn, they’ll never actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.600
release they’re quote on quote “revolutionary&nbsp;
product or service”. And that’s just two examples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:32.080
52% of all DotCom companies went bankrupt by&nbsp;
2004, and there’s no doubt that that included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:37.520
dozens of brilliant ideas. So, clearly, simply&nbsp;
investing in a company with a good idea doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:45.840
guarantee investment growth. In fact, during&nbsp;
the dotcom bubble, most of the time it didn’t.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.560
THE SURVIVORS:
Ok, so betting on unprofitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.680
disruptors is basically just a gamble. But what&nbsp;
if you’re not investing in the unprofitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.880
disruptors like Rivian, Lucid, Teledoc,&nbsp;
and Nanodimension. What if you’re investing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:04.240
in profitable disruptors like Tesla, Shopify,&nbsp;
and Square. These companies will most likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.480
survive a multi-year bear market with no&nbsp;
additional funding. So, buying the dips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:14.240
on these companies is relatively safe right?&nbsp;
Well, if you hold long enough, then probably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.880
but you’re also likely looking at several&nbsp;
painful years ahead. For example, take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.000
look at dotcom favorites that actually survived&nbsp;
and ended up becoming big. Before Amazon became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.400
a trillion-dollar e-commerce giant and liberals&nbsp;
started hating Jeff Bezos, Amazon went through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:36.160
the dotcom crash. And Amazon didn’t sell of 40&nbsp;
or 50 or even 70%. Amazon sold off a painful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:42.000
95%. At that point, you’d probably be questioning&nbsp;
if the company is gonna survive regardless of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.520
the balance sheet looks. But, even if you stayed&nbsp;
invested, you would’ve had to wait 7 years just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.800
almost breakeven. And then, the 2008 financial&nbsp;
recession would hit, and you’d be down 70% on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:58.720
your initial investment once again. It wasn’t till&nbsp;
November of 2009 or 10 years after the dot-com&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:04.320
peak that you would actually start making money on&nbsp;
Amazon. Another great example is Booking holdings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.400
Booking holdings was a travel booking company&nbsp;
that went hyperbolic during the dotcom bubble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.840
and it’s still around today and it’s doing better&nbsp;
than ever before. But like with all companies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.640
were founded during the dotcom bubble, it had to&nbsp;
go through the dotcom crash first. And this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:25.600
we’re not even talking about a 95% crash, we’re&nbsp;
talking about a 99.36% crash. This means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:31.520
for every $100 you invested into Booking holdings,&nbsp;
you would have just 64 cents or not even a dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:37.440
left at the bottom of the crash. And it took&nbsp;
Booking Holdings 14 years just to recover to its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:43.920
dot-com peak. Also, it should be noted that even&nbsp;
today, while Booking Holdings is at $2000 a share,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.120
that’s only a 2X from its dot-com peak meaning&nbsp;
that it took 25 years to just double your money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.400
Even just investing in the S&amp;P 500 would’ve&nbsp;
given you a better return, not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:00.160
you would’ve never been down 99.36%. So,&nbsp;
while you may not lose money if you go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.840
all-in on a disruptor that survives, you could&nbsp;
easily be waiting a decade just to break even.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.040
STUCK FOREVER:&nbsp;

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:14.640
Ok, so disruptors are dangerous. You’re could&nbsp;
either lose it all or you could end up waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:20.080
several years just to breakeven. But, what if you&nbsp;
don’t even mess with disruptor or growth stocks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:24.160
What if you’re a blue-chip investor that only&nbsp;
invests in the largest and most stable companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:30.080
in the world like Apple, Microsoft, and Google?&nbsp;
Is it ok to go all-in on dips in this scenario?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:36.240
Well, not really. In fact, I’d say this is worse&nbsp;
than going all-in on a profitable disruptor. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.400
profitable disruptors, you can be sure that if&nbsp;
the company is able to weather the downturn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.280
they’re probably going to the moon once the&nbsp;
market recovers. The same cannot be said about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:49.280
slower-moving large-cap companies. Here’s the&nbsp;
thing, while we like to often complain about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.680
companies being too powerful and having too&nbsp;
big of a monopoly, companies don’t stay at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:58.640
their peak forever. In fact, if you’re gonna be&nbsp;
deterministic about anything in the stock market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:03.920
it should be that companies come and go. And&nbsp;
with the rise of technology and rapid disruption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:08.960
the lifespans of companies are getting shorter and&nbsp;
shorter. In fact, Mckinsey &amp; Company found that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:14.800
the average time a company stays in the S&amp;P 500 is&nbsp;
just 18 years. Considering this, they predict that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:20.640
75% of companies that are currently part of the&nbsp;
S&amp;P 500 will have disappeared by 2027. Also, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:25.520
if they don’t disappear, it’s very likely that&nbsp;
they become a shell of their former self. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:31.600
2000s, the companies that dominated the fortune&nbsp;
500 were GE, Exxon, Proctor &amp; Gamble, DuPont,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.200
and so on and so forth. While these companies are&nbsp;
still around, disruptors like Apple, Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.840
and Amazon have grown to become 10 times larger.&nbsp;
But, wait a minute companies like market giants of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.640
the past like GE and Exxon weren’t tech giants,&nbsp;
so they had a tough time keeping up with tech.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:52.320
Companies like Apple and Google should have&nbsp;
a far better shot at keeping up right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.360
this is not exactly true either and the best&nbsp;
example of this is Cisco. During the dotcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.400
bubble, it wasn’t just speculative companies that&nbsp;
got bid up, but also the biggest tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:07.440
in the world. At the peak of the dotcom bubble,&nbsp;
Cisco became the world’s largest company boasting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:15.040
a market cap of $500 billion. If we adjust for&nbsp;
inflation, that’s $823 billion today. Since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:20.880
22 years have passed, yet Cisco is yet to put in&nbsp;
a new all-time high. In fact, it’s currently still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:25.760
32% away from its dot-com peak, and that’s with&nbsp;
Cisco doing a bunch of stock buybacks and trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:30.560
to soften the downfall. If we just look at their&nbsp;
market cap, it’s currently less than $250 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:36.560
meaning that the stock has to run over 100% just&nbsp;
to reach their peak market cap from 22 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:42.000
and that’s not even accounting for inflation.&nbsp;
Cisco isn’t just a one-off exception either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:47.840
Other top 10 companies that never recovered&nbsp;
include Intel and AT&amp;T. Who’s to say that Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:54.080
isn’t the next Cisco? I mean have seen the last&nbsp;
4 generations of iPhones? Only time will tell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:58.640
but just keep in mind that going all-in on&nbsp;
tech giants may not be as safe as you think.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.720
INDEX INVESTOR:&nbsp;

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:06.160
And finally, that brings us&nbsp;
to the index fund investor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:11.120
If you’re gonna constantly go all-in on every dip,&nbsp;
indices are definitely what you want to be buying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:16.560
but I suspect that most people who are FOMOing&nbsp;
into stocks aren’t FOMOing into index funds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:21.600
With that being said though, it’s not exactly&nbsp;
a good idea to FOMO into index funds either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.880
Take the Nasdaq for example. After the dotcom&nbsp;
bubble burst, the Nasdaq crashed a whopping 83%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:32.720
Keep in mind that the Nasdaq is a combination of&nbsp;
100 of the largest and most profitable companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.080
on the market. Yet, it was able to crash&nbsp;
83% within a few years, and it took a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:43.360
and drawn-out 17 years to return to the dot-com&nbsp;
peak. Many of you guys watching probably aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:49.040
even 17 years old. If you invested in the S&amp;P&nbsp;
500 instead, you would’ve had a better time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:54.080
but not by much. First, you would’ve experienced&nbsp;
a 50% crash along with a 7 year-long wait to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:58.160
return to all-time highs. Just when you think&nbsp;
things are finally going to go bullish again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.800
you’re hit with a 57% crash that takes you&nbsp;
to lower levels than the dotcom crash. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:07.360
it’s not till 13 years after your initial&nbsp;
investment that you finally start to make money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:12.000
So, as you can see, if you go all-in at the&nbsp;
wrong time, you could still easily be stuck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:16.240
for well over a decade even with an index&nbsp;
fund. But, at least you can be sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:21.440
index funds will recover sooner or later right?&nbsp;
Well, Michael Burry seems to think that index&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:25.920
funds are actually in some sort of crazy bubble&nbsp;
themselves due to the rise of passive investing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:31.840
and that index funds are doomed to crash and not&nbsp;
recover. But that’s a whole nother discussion.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:34.640
COURSE OF ACTION:&nbsp;

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:39.600
So far, we’ve gone through several examples and&nbsp;
hypotheticals, but the lesson at the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:46.160
day is to just not FOMO into the market. Just&nbsp;
because the market dips 10 or 15 or 20% doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:51.040
mean that you should bet the farm on an index or&nbsp;
any individual stock. This type of behavior is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:56.000
what leads to people resenting the stock market&nbsp;
and missing the best buying opportunity of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.400
decade. By the time the stock market actually&nbsp;
bottoms, they’ve either stopped investing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:05.440
or they ran out of serious capital a long time&nbsp;
ago because they just kept buying every dip.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:10.960
So, what should you do instead? Well, this is&nbsp;
not financial advice, but the smartest course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:15.520
of action in my opinion is simply a long-term&nbsp;
weighted dollar-cost averaging strategy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:21.120
Maybe every week, you invest $100 into the S&amp;P&nbsp;
500. This way, regardless of what the market is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:26.240
doing, you have at least some money going into the&nbsp;
market. This is extremely important because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:30.720
only risk that’s bigger than FOMOing in at the&nbsp;
top is the risk you’re taking by not investing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:36.720
at all. Anyway, going back to the plan, if the&nbsp;
S&amp;P 500 is down over 10% from its all-time high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:42.240
maybe you could cut back on spending and invest&nbsp;
$200 per week. And if it’s down over 20%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:46.720
maybe you could live on sticks and bones and&nbsp;
invest $300 a week and so on and so forth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:51.200
That’s just one example of what you could do,&nbsp;
but there are several great long-term strategies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:55.360
that you can implement. It doesn’t really matter&nbsp;
which one you choose as long as you stick to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:59.760
don’t let emotions alter your plans, and&nbsp;
most importantly don’t be deterministic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:05.200
about anything as no investment is too big&nbsp;
to fall. But that’s just what I think. Have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:09.120
you guys felt any FOMO in regards to the stock&nbsp;
market within the last two years? Comment that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:14.320
down below. Also, drop a like this video helped&nbsp;
you realize how brutal the stock market can be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:18.000
And of course, consider checking out our&nbsp;
international channels to watch our videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:21.760
in other languages and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

